    "updated_at": "2026-08-11T22:39:05Z",
    "body": "Checking my own understanding here.\n\nIn Fonts 4:\n* The 'font' longhands are all font-affecting\n* So are the 'font-synthesis' longhands, I think? Assuming that, say, synthetic bold might adjust the ch width or something.\n* Are the [font-feature properties](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-fonts-4/#font-rend-props)? Hmm, I guess they can affect which glyph is used for 0, and thus the ch width too. font-variant-east-asian could affect `ic`, too. And I guess the generic `font-variant` can affect both of them in theory.\n* I guess font-optical-sizing is too?\n* And font-language-override, as it could affect the glyph used for `ic`.\n* font-variant-emoji is *not* font-affecting, the affected characters aren't relied on by font-relative lengths nor are we likely to add font-relative lengths that are in the future reliant on those characters (or we can define them to be equal to the image variant, if we ever want to expose an emoji-width unit)\n* Nor is font-palette, as it's just colors\n\nFonts 5 doesn't introduce any relevant new properties.\n\nMathML will need to define that math-depth is font-affecting.\n\nAnd that's it, maybe?",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
